body:
I saw alloca for the first time in the BVH and thought it was possibly the coolest single thing I have seen yet in cpp. So I am keen to use it sometime, but I think it might be overkill here, and in the worst case (as you mentioned) you will have blown the stack out of cache and maybe the worst case gets worse. Similar issue for a static LocalVector, because you are processing through more memory (the HashMap and LocalVector)

The loop (as written) will technically only do 1.5 passes on average, because when an item is processed on the first pass, it is also removed from the HashMap so it doesn't show up on the second pass.

For every element in this list, there will have been at least one broadphase collision test and a narrow phase collision test that was run within this same physics frame. So this traversal is a pretty small part of the overall cost of an element arriving in this HashMap.

The HashMap looks as fast as I would hope for (for iteration), with all the elements chained in a simple linked list. See excerpt from it's Iterator below:

 ```cpp
	struct Iterator {
		_FORCE_INLINE_ KeyValue<TKey, TValue> &operator*() const {
			return E->data;
		}
		_FORCE_INLINE_ KeyValue<TKey, TValue> *operator->() const { return &E->data; }
		_FORCE_INLINE_ Iterator &operator++() {
			if (E) {
				E = E->next;
			}
			return *this;
		}
```

One cost that I don't love is the HashMap remove() function. When I call remove() with an Iterator, it feels to me like it should be able to just remove the element immediately, since the Iterator already holds a valid reference to the element to be removed. However it instead grabs the key from the Iterator and does a fresh lookup, and then removes it. This seems costly, but there is probably a good reason it is necessary.

```cpp
	_FORCE_INLINE_ void remove(const Iterator &p_iter) {
		if (p_iter) {
			erase(p_iter->key);
		}
	}
```

In any case, by removing elements one at a time within the loop, we reduce the second pass work to it's minimum, and it may actually be better (in the worst case) to remove the element while it is in cache, than to do a HashMap.clear() after the loop (which would run through the list again deallocating each member). Since the existing code was already doing the remove() within the loop, I thought I would leave it that way.
